# 4DIP Physics Examples (11–20)

## 11. Orbital Mechanics (Circular Orbit)

This example demonstrates the motion of a **satellite in a circular orbit** around Earth.

- **Equation**: 
  \[
  F = \frac{GMm}{r^2} = \frac{mv^2}{r}
  \]
- **Description**: The force acting on an object in orbit is due to gravity, and it is balanced by the centripetal force. This example calculates the orbital velocity for a circular orbit.

**Calculations**:  
Given:
- Gravitational constant \( G = 6.67430 \times 10^{-11} \, \text{m}^3 \, \text{kg}^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-2} \)
- Mass of Earth \( M = 5.972 \times 10^{24} \, \text{kg} \)
- Mass of satellite \( m = 1000 \, \text{kg} \)
- Orbital radius \( r = 7000 \, \text{km} \)

\[
v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}} = \sqrt{\frac{(6.67430 \times 10^{-11})(5.972 \times 10^{24})}{7000 \times 10^3}} = 7.577 \, \text{km/s}
\]

- **Final Velocity**: \( 7.577 \, \text{km/s} \)
- **Error**: \( \pm 0.01 \, \text{km/s} \)
- **Relative Error**: \( 0.13 \% \)

