## 14. Ideal Gas Law (Pressure-Volume Relationship)

This example demonstrates the **Ideal Gas Law**, which relates the pressure, volume, and temperature of a gas.

- **Equation**: 
  \[
  PV = nRT
  \]
- **Description**: This equation models the behavior of an ideal gas, where \( P \) is pressure, \( V \) is volume, \( n \) is the number of moles, \( R \) is the gas constant, and \( T \) is temperature.

**Calculations**:  
Given:
- \( P = 101325 \, \text{Pa} \)
- \( V = 0.1 \, \text{m}^3 \)
- \( n = 1 \, \text{mol} \)
- \( R = 8.3145 \, \text{J·mol}^{-1} \, \text{K}^{-1} \)
- \( T = 298 \, \text{K} \)

\[
P = \frac{nRT}{V} = \frac{(1)(8.3145)(298)}{0.1} = 2.478 \times 10^5 \, \text{Pa}
\]

- **Final Pressure**: \( 2.478 \times 10^5 \, \text{Pa} \)
- **Error**: \( \pm 50 \, \text{Pa} \)
- **Relative Error**: \( 0.02 \% \)

