## 16. Brownian Motion (Particle Displacement)

This example demonstrates **Brownian motion**, showing the random movement of particles in a fluid.

- **Equation**: 
  \[
  \langle x^2 \rangle = \frac{2 k_B T}{\gamma} t
  \]
- **Description**: The mean square displacement of a particle in Brownian motion is proportional to the time, with constants for temperature and damping.

**Calculations**:  
Given:
- Boltzmann constant \( k_B = 1.380649 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{J/K} \)
- Temperature \( T = 300 \, \text{K} \)
- Damping coefficient \( \gamma = 1.0 \, \text{kg/s} \)
- Time \( t = 10.0 \, \text{s} \)

\[
\langle x^2 \rangle = \frac{(2)(1.380649 \times 10^{-23})(300)}{1} \times 10.0 = 8.29 \times 10^{-21} \, \text{m}^2
\]

- **Final Displacement**: \( 8.29 \times 10^{-21} \, \text{m}^2 \)
- **Error**: \( \pm 1 \times 10^{-23} \, \text{m}^2 \)
- **Relative Error**: \( 1.2 \% \)

