## 17. Nuclear Decay (Half-Life)

This example models the **exponential decay** of a radioactive substance.

- **Equation**: 
  \[
  N(t) = N_0 e^{-\lambda t}
  \]
- **Description**: The number of remaining nuclei \( N(t) \) decreases exponentially over time, where \( \lambda \) is the decay constant.

**Calculations**:  
Given:
- Initial quantity \( N_0 = 1000 \)
- Decay constant \( \lambda = 0.693 \, \text{hr}^{-1} \)
- Time \( t = 10.0 \, \text{hours} \)

\[
N(t) = 1000 \cdot e^{-0.693 \times 10} = 1000 \cdot e^{-6.93} = 0.001 \, \text{nuclei}
\]

- **Final Quantity**: \( 0.001 \, \text{nuclei} \)
- **Error**: \( \pm 0.01 \, \text{nuclei} \)
- **Relative Error**: \( 1 \% \)

